From c0827a4b065240d44bba63582dbc3bc4c0df0644 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ObeseCatLord <6099645+ObeseCatLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 07:33:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] vu1: exact flag liveness, lazy block cycles, vector flag
 results, leaner dispatch

Callgrind (generated path, ga2_vu1_check --repeat 1; eigou-2 narration / zettai
map): 592.5M -> 330.0M Ir (-44.3%) and 543.6M -> 300.2M (-44.8%), from 71.6 to
39.9 host instructions per VU cycle on narration. --reference: 0 mismatches on
narration, map, battle and tutorial; ps2x_tests 579/579. Steps, in order
(narration / map Ir after the step):

- MAC liveness (ga2_vu1_plan.h): a producer is dead when a later producer is
  already four pairs old at every reader (before: the latest one had to be),
  so 80 of 234 flag-producing pairs in the big program drop to 24; a D/T halt
  ends the run only if the unit has the bit enabled (it counts like an E bit
  and the producer at/after a halt stays live), and the control flow is
  analysed with a pair entered as a delay slot apart from one entered by a
  jump (a slot that is also a jump target followed the wrong successors).
  504.6M / 462.6M.
- Blocks: the cycle counter is only written by pairs that look at it or at
  what the FDIV/EFU pipelines commit (pairNeedsCycle, a whitelist of ops that
  do not), plus the pipeline commits due by then, and by finishBlock; no
  per-cycle advance or commit poll. Direct jumps between blocks (static branch
  targets; tail calls between chunks, musttail on clang), no code-size check
  after a block, VF/ACC ready cycles that are complete when the block ends are
  not stored. (A non-inlined pairNeedsCycle cost +40% until forced inline.)
  435.2M / 399.4M.
- CLIP liveness (kFlagClip): a CLIP superseded by a later clip write four
  pairs old at every clip reader only shifts the clip history. 425.3M / 390.6M.
- FMAC flag results: MAC bits for the flagged pairs are computed four lanes at
  once (ADD/SUB/MUL, accumulating forms, OPMSUB/OPMULA) instead of per lane
  with branches and long double fallbacks; the flag entry is queued inline.
  391.6M / 359.6M.
- Normal-range fast paths: results whose exponent is in [1, 253] (single
  rounding) or [61, 253] with an unsaturated product (accumulating forms)
  need no clamping and have only a sign flag; proved in the code comments.
  369.2M / 339.6M.
- Block guards on VF/VI/ACC registers at offset >= 3 hold by construction
  (writes complete four cycles after issue) and are not emitted; no
  m_state.cycles store per block. 346.7M / 319.4M.
- Chunks of 128 pairs (was 48) and no stack protector in them: fewer
  hand-overs. Costs ~60% more compile time for the programs. 329.2M / 299.4M.
- Generated programs start or resume at any pair that expects what is pending
  (kFacts per pair; registerJitEntry gets the table) instead of interpreting
  until nothing is pending: a chunk boundary between a branch and its delay
  slot no longer costs a generic step (880 Ir each, 3% of narration at 48-pair
  chunks). 330.0M / 300.2M at 128-pair chunks.

Fix found by the fuzzer, present before: the compiler merged "restore the
lanes a pair does not write" into vector arithmetic (a multiply by 1.0), which
quieted signaling-NaN bit patterns that VU registers also hold as integers
(ITOF with a partial dest). Lane copies now move bits (ga2_vu1::copyLane).

Tools: ga2_vu1_fuzz (ps2xTest) emits random programs and compares the
interpreter with generated programs, or with an interpreter that only computes
some flag results (--flags), over random states with resumes, GIF packets and
every float class; GA2_VU1GEN_HITDUMP gives per-pair execution counts.
ga2_vu1_plan.h holds the generator's analyses (flag liveness, entry facts,
blocks) so the fuzzer runs the same code.

Verification: ga2_vu1_check --reference x3 on the four captures: 0 mismatches;
ps2x_tests 579/579; the interpreter's digests (state, VU memory, GIF, cycles)
are identical to b86ec90 on 1.9M random runs, and its replay of the recorded
digests of the real captures gives the same results as b86ec90; generated
programs vs the interpreter on 540 random images (3.7M random-state runs) and
on 70 more with 16-pair chunks (0.65M): 0 mismatches; candidates that only
compute some flag results (--flags 1..6) vs the full interpreter: 0
mismatches (mutated fast paths are caught within seconds). Not checked in the
game itself.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
